# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Body text of the report. Each section = list of (kind, content). kinds: p, h2, bullets, table, fig, caption
ABSTRACT = (
"One-time-password (OTP) scams are among the most frequent financial frauds reported in Bhutan. A caller promises a prize, persuades the victim to read out SMS codes, and within minutes the banking app is bound to another phone and the account is empty. This study asks what the victim's Android phone can prove afterwards. We (1) reviewed Bhutanese news, police and bank statements and seven peer-reviewed papers, (2) modelled the scam as an eight-step attack flow, (3) built a fictitious victim phone image containing schema-faithful SMS, call-log, WhatsApp, usage-statistics, notification and mock-banking-app data, and (4) acquired, hashed and analysed it with ALEAPP and SQLite tooling. The reconstructed timeline covers 46 events between 13:58 and 14:20 Bhutan Time and shows that the scam call (6 min 12 s) overlapped both OTP messages and both debits, that the victim's own banking app was not in use while the money left, and that the phone cannot directly prove the single most important act: the voice disclosure of the code. Only bank-side logs can show which device used the OTP. We conclude with a short preservation checklist for victims and logging requirements for banks and telecom operators, and we state plainly the limits of an emulator-free, synthetic experiment."
)

SECTIONS = []
S = SECTIONS.append

# ---------------- 1 INTRODUCTION
S(("1. Introduction", [
("p","Mobile banking has moved quickly in Bhutan. Wallets such as goBoB and apps such as mBoB and mPay now let customers move money without visiting a branch, and Bank of Bhutan alone reported more than Nu 1.4 billion in goBoB transactions in 2023 (The Bhutanese, 2024a). The same convenience has opened a path for fraud. Bank officials describe a recurring pattern in which a caller, often on WhatsApp, says the victim has won a lottery or is about to receive money, collects an account number and citizen identity number, and then asks for the OTP that the bank sends by SMS (BBS, n.d.-b; The Bhutanese, n.d.-a). Bank of Bhutan's information security head explained that with the OTP a scammer can register the app on a new device, reset the MPIN and \"clean out\" the account (BBS, n.d.-b)."),
("p","Police statistics suggest the problem is growing and diversifying. In the first three months of 2026 the Royal Bhutan Police (RBP) recorded 35 scam cases involving more than Nu 5.4 million, of which only about Nu 830,000 had been recovered; 16 were email-compromise or mobile \"blackout\" (SIM-replacement) cases, and the remainder included lottery prizes and OTP sharing (BBS, 2026). When a victim reports such a case, investigators ask a practical question: what can the victim's phone prove?"),
("p","This study addresses that question through a controlled recreation using fictitious data. It is organised around three research questions."),
("bullets",["RQ1. What steps do OTP scams follow in Bhutan, and where does each step leave digital traces?",
"RQ2. Which artefacts on the victim's Android phone (SMS, call log, WhatsApp, notifications, app usage) can rebuild the timeline, and which steps leave no artefact on the phone at all?",
"RQ3. What have Bhutanese banks and telecom operators changed since these scams began, and what gaps remain?"]),
("p","The contribution is a minute-by-minute reconstruction in which every step is tied to a named artefact, a documented chain of custody for the evidence, and practical recommendations for victims, banks and telecom operators. All people, numbers, accounts and amounts in the experiment are invented; no real bank, customer or scammer data were used."),
]))

# ---------------- 2 LITERATURE REVIEW
S(("2. Literature Review", [
("p","Local context. Bhutanese reporting is largely journalistic and institutional rather than academic, so it is treated here as primary descriptive evidence. Reports agree on the core sequence of lure, phone contact, request for account details and then the OTP (BBS, n.d.-a; The Bhutanese, n.d.-a). Bank of Bhutan explained that the mBoB app allowed the MPIN and the registered-device identifier (IMEI) to be reset after OTP validation, which is why a single disclosed code could hand over the account (BBS, n.d.-b). Scam variants documented locally include fake visa-office callers (BBS, n.d.-c), recycled SIM numbers that left a traveller's account exposed (The Bhutanese, n.d.-b), phishing emails linked to bank accounts, and sudden handset \"blackouts\" after a fraudulent SIM replacement (BBS, 2026; The Bhutanese, 2026). Peer-reviewed work on Bhutan specifically is, to our knowledge, absent, so we draw on international research."),
("p","Weaknesses of SMS OTPs. Mulliner et al. (2013) argued that treating SMS as a secure channel is mistaken, showing that malware on the handset can read or forward messages and proposing an operating-system-level dedicated channel as a defence. Dmitrienko et al. (2014) analysed deployed mobile two-factor schemes and found that when the second factor is delivered to the same smartphone that runs the account, a compromise or a design flaw can defeat it. The Bhutanese scam does not need malware at all, because the victim relays the code, but both papers support the same design lesson: an OTP proves only that someone with access to the channel received it."),
("p","Social engineering of OTPs. Siadati et al. (2017) tested a social-engineering attack that asks users to forward their verification codes and reported a 50% success rate against a standard SMS verification message. Their most robust redesign of the message cut success to about 8%. This is directly relevant to RQ3: the Bhutanese banks' messages tell customers not to share codes, and the study suggests that message design materially changes how many people comply."),
("p","SIM swapping. Lee et al. (2020) tested the SIM-swap procedures of five U.S. prepaid carriers using 50 accounts and found that all five used authentication challenges that an attacker could subvert, and that 17 websites could be compromised by a SIM swap alone. Their findings frame the \"blackout\" cases reported by the RBP, where scammers pose as the customer, obtain a replacement SIM and then receive the bank's OTPs (BBS, 2026). The evidential consequence is that the victim's phone loses network and the OTPs never reach it, so SMS artefacts will be missing rather than present."),
("p","Mobile banking app security. Reaves et al. (2017) examined all 46 known Android mobile-money applications and reported pervasive vulnerabilities, including faulty certificate validation and home-made cryptography, together with terms of service that place liability mainly on customers. This matters for evidence as well as safety: weak client-side design and thin logging both reduce what a victim can show."),
("p","Android forensics. Anglano (2014) documented the artefacts WhatsApp leaves on Android, including the message and contact databases, and showed how they can be correlated to establish who communicated with whom and when; the study used an emulator validated against real handsets. Azfar et al. (2016) extracted data from 30 popular Android communication apps and produced a taxonomy of recoverable artefact classes. Together they justify our decision to rely on the application databases that ALEAPP parses. National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) guidance on mobile device forensics stresses preservation, documentation and tool validation (Ayers et al., 2014), while NIST digital identity guidelines classify SMS-delivered out-of-band codes as a restricted authenticator because of the risks above (Grassi et al., 2017)."),
("p","Gap. The literature explains why OTP and SIM channels fail and how Android apps can be examined, but none of the studies reviewed reconstructs a complete OTP-sharing takeover from the victim's handset and then compares it with what the bank must supply. That comparison is the focus of this study."),
]))

# ---------------- 3 METHODOLOGY
S(("3. Methodology", [
("p","Design. The study is a controlled, reproducible digital-forensic experiment with a literature and source review. The workflow was: (1) review sources; (2) design a scenario and an attack flow; (3) build the victim phone data; (4) acquire and hash it; (5) analyse with forensic tools; (6) merge artefacts into one timeline; and (7) derive recommendations."),
("p","Sources. Bhutanese news (BBS, The Bhutanese), RBP statements and bank statements quoted in them were read for scam steps, losses and countermeasures. Seven peer-reviewed papers (Section 2) and two NIST publications were reviewed. Publication dates of several Bhutanese articles could not be confirmed from the web pages and are cited as \"n.d.\"; the statistics quoted are therefore reported as stated by the outlets and were not independently verified."),
("p","Scenario. A fictitious victim, \"Pema Dema (TEST)\", uses a Pixel-class phone with the number +975 17 000 001. On 12 November 2026 (Bhutan Time, UTC+6) a fictitious scammer contacts her on WhatsApp about a lucky-draw prize, phones her, and uses two OTPs sent to her phone to bind the mock bank account \"TEST-0001\" (balance Nu 50,000) to a second device and move the money out. The script, times and message texts are in Appendix C."),
("p","Phone data. The brief asked for an Android emulator. A full emulator image could not be downloaded on the available connection (measured at roughly 60 KB/s against a system image of about 1.5 GB), so this study did not run one. Instead, a Python script (scripts/build_evidence.py) generates a file-system extraction that follows real Android paths and schemas: mmssms.db (Telephony provider), calllog.db (Contacts provider, including the deleted column that ALEAPP requires), WhatsApp msgstore.db and wa.db (current message, jid and chat tables), a UsageStats event log written as a real IntervalStatsProto protobuf using ALEAPP's own protobuf definitions, a JSON notification log, and the database of a mock banking app. The mock app, MockBank, was written in Kotlin (mockbank-app/) but was not compiled or run, because no Android SDK was available; its database schema is populated by the same script. A separate CSV simulates the bank's server log, which the police would have to request. Messages were not exchanged between live WhatsApp accounts; they were inserted directly. This limits ecological validity and is discussed in Section 5."),
("p","Acquisition and integrity. The extraction was archived as a tar file and every file was hashed with SHA-256 (Appendix B). The hashes were recalculated after analysis and all eight matched, indicating the analysis did not alter the evidence. ALEAPP opens databases read-only."),
("p","Analysis tools. ALEAPP (build 2026.4.6-dev, run in file-system mode) parsed SMS, call logs, WhatsApp chats, WhatsApp calls and usage statistics, producing HTML, TSV and LAVA outputs. The mock-bank database, which no standard parser knows, was examined with SQLite queries, and a Python script (scripts/build_timeline.py) merged all sources into one table. Autopsy was not used because its installation was not feasible on the connection available; DB Browser for SQLite is substituted by the sqlite3 command-line client, which executes the same SQL (Appendix D). The notification log was read with a custom parser, not ALEAPP's notification-history module, since the real protobuf store was not generated."),
("p","Time handling. Android stores times in UTC epoch milliseconds and ALEAPP reports UTC. All timeline entries were converted to Bhutan Time (UTC+6), and device-versus-bank clock skew is assumed to be zero because both are simulated; in a real case clock accuracy would have to be tested."),
("p","Ethics. Only invented data were created. No real accounts, numbers or people were involved, no live system was attacked, and no real scammer techniques beyond those publicly reported were operationalised."),
]))

# ---------------- 4 EXPERIMENT AND FINDINGS
S(("4. Experiment and Findings", [
("h2","4.1 Attack flow and evidence points (RQ1)"),
("p","Figure 1 summarises the eight steps derived from the sources and the artefact each leaves. Steps 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7 and 8 leave at least one trace; step 4, the disclosure of the OTP, is spoken and leaves none."),
("fig","fig1_attack_flow.png","Figure 1. Attack flow from first contact to cash-out, with evidence created at each step."),
("h2","4.2 Artefacts recovered (RQ2)"),
("p","ALEAPP recovered every ALEAPP-supported artefact in the extraction without errors, with one exception: a WhatsApp extended-data query failed because a poll table that newer WhatsApp versions create was absent from the synthetic database (analysis_run.log). That failure did not affect the chat or call tables used here. Table 1 lists what each source contained and what it can establish."),
("table",1,"Table 1. Artefacts examined, tool, record counts and evidential value",
 ["Artefact (path)","Tool","Records","What it establishes"],
 [["SMS (mmssms.db)","ALEAPP","5","Arrival time of both OTPs, the new-device alert and both debit alerts; wording shows the OTP's purpose"],
  ["Call log (calllog.db)","ALEAPP","3","Incoming call from scammer number 14:03:45, 372 s; outgoing call to bank 14:16:20; unanswered call back 14:20:05"],
  ["WhatsApp (msgstore.db, wa.db)","ALEAPP","7 chat, 1 call","Lure at 13:58:30, instructions, post-theft message \"ok sir i gave the code\", warning to friend; one unanswered call at 14:02:10"],
  ["UsageStats (protobuf)","ALEAPP","12 events","Dialer in foreground 14:03:44 to 14:10:00; MockBank only at 14:13:15"],
  ["Notification log (JSON)","Custom script","6","Notifications shown within 1 s of each SMS; lock-screen exposure of OTP text"],
  ["MockBank (mockbank.db)","SQLite","3 auth, 2 OTP, 1 binding","Victim device rejected as unbound at 14:13:15; MPIN rejected at 14:13:40"],
  ["Bank server log (CSV, off-phone)","Manual","7","Attacker device, IP, OTP verify times, rebinding at 14:08:02, transfers at 14:09:09 and 14:09:47"]]),
("h2","4.3 Minute-by-minute timeline"),
("p","The merged timeline (analysis/master_timeline_BTT.csv) contains 46 events. Figure 2 plots them by source and Table 2 gives the decisive entries. The scam call began at 14:03:45 and, by its duration of 372 s, ended at about 14:09:57."),
("fig","fig2_timeline.png","Figure 2. Swim-lane timeline of all recovered events (Bhutan Time, 12 November 2026, test data)."),
("table",2,"Table 2. Key events and the artefact that proves each (Bhutan Time)",
 ["Time","Event","Proof (artefact)"],
 [["13:58:30","Prize lure received on WhatsApp","msgstore.db message row, sender 97577000222"],
  ["14:03:45","Call from the same number begins (372 s)","calllog.db, type Incoming; UsageStats dialer foreground 14:03:44"],
  ["14:04:48","Attacker requests device-registration OTP","Bank server log (not on phone)"],
  ["14:04:50","OTP #1 SMS arrives (65 s into the call)","mmssms.db row 1; notification 14:04:51"],
  ["14:05:35","OTP #1 verified from device Redmi-X, IP 203.0.113.45","Bank server log only"],
  ["14:06:40","OTP #2 (MPIN reset) arrives","mmssms.db row 2; mockbank.db otp_log"],
  ["14:08:02","Account bound to new device; victim device revoked","SMS alert row 3; bank log DEVICE_BOUND"],
  ["14:09:10","Debit Nu 30,000 (balance Nu 20,000)","mmssms.db row 4; bank log 14:09:09"],
  ["14:09:48","Debit Nu 19,950 (balance Nu 50)","mmssms.db row 5; bank log 14:09:47"],
  ["14:10:20","Victim writes \"ok sir i gave the code\"","msgstore.db outgoing message"],
  ["14:13:15","Victim opens MockBank: device unbound","mockbank.db auth_events; UsageStats"],
  ["14:16:20","Victim phones bank hotline (241 s)","calllog.db outgoing"]]),
("h2","4.4 What the timeline shows"),
("bullets",[
"Speed. Cash-out began 67 s after the new-device activation (14:08:02 to 14:09:09) and the whole takeover from first OTP request to last debit took 5 min 0 s (14:04:48 to 14:09:48). From first contact to last debit was 11 min 18 s.",
"Overlap. Both OTP SMS and both debits fall inside the 372-second call, so the call-log row corroborates that the victim was on the phone with the same number that had contacted her on WhatsApp when the codes arrived.",
"Latency. SMS arrival followed the bank's OTP request by about 2 s, so the SMS timestamp can be used to bracket the server-side event when the bank log is unavailable.",
"Absence of use. UsageStats shows no MockBank foreground activity between the earlier 09:12 use and 14:13:15, which supports the claim that the transfers were not made on the victim's handset. The mock app's own record that its binding was revoked carries the time of the next app open (14:13:15), not the real revocation time (14:08:02), showing why client-side logs under-report.",
"Exposure. The notification log shows OTP text displayed in notifications within a second, which would also expose it to anyone looking at the lock screen or, in other attack types, to a notification-reading app.",
"The gap. No artefact on the phone records that the victim read the codes aloud. The inference rests on three facts together: the call overlapped both OTP arrivals, the bank log shows both codes were verified from another device 45 s and 70 s after delivery, and the victim's later message admits sharing the code."]),
("h2","4.5 Evidence the phone cannot supply"),
("p","In this scenario the phone cannot prove who initiated the OTP requests, from which device or address they came, whether the transfers were authenticated by the OTP or by the MPIN, or where the money went. Those facts exist only in the bank's server log (Table 2, rows 3 and 5). In the SIM-replacement variant the position is worse: the handset loses service, so the OTP SMS never arrive and the phone holds only the loss-of-signal event and the victim's later reports (BBS, 2026)."),
]))

# ---------------- 5 DISCUSSION
S(("5. Discussion", [
("h2","5.1 What Bhutanese banks and telecoms have changed (RQ3)"),
("p","Public statements describe several responses. Banks removed in-app self-service resets: after the first wave of cases banks reported that password and MPIN resets and the device identifier reset had been disabled in the app, so that customers now call the bank or visit a branch and complete forms (BBS, n.d.-b). Banks send bulk SMS warnings that staff will never request OTPs or app PINs, the Royal Monetary Authority (RMA) has told the public not to lend, share or rent accounts, and the Department of Law and Order issued a public notification on 9 February 2024 (BBS, n.d.-c). Bhutan National Bank states that disputed amounts are frozen on report and reimbursed after investigation (The Bhutanese, n.d.-a), and inter-bank cooperation has allowed funds that stayed inside Bhutan to be blocked, unlike funds sent to India or elsewhere (BBS, n.d.-a). For SIM-related cases Bhutan Telecom explained that SIMs not recharged within three months expire and are recycled, and introduced a retention service of up to three years for people living abroad (The Bhutanese, n.d.-b). In 2026 the RBP, RMA, financial institutions and GovTech were exploring de-linking email addresses from bank accounts (BBS, 2026)."),
("table",3,"Table 3. Responses reported and the gaps our experiment suggests",
 ["Response (source)","Gap that remains"],
 [["Self-service MPIN and device reset disabled (BBS, n.d.-b)","Registration OTP flow in our scenario still allowed binding; the control is only as strong as the weakest remaining path"],
  ["Warnings by SMS and press (BBS, n.d.-c)","Warning text sits beside the code; it does not say what the code does, and victims under live pressure still comply (cf. Siadati et al., 2017)"],
  ["Freeze and reimburse after report (The Bhutanese, n.d.-a)","Speed decides outcome: the money left in 5 min, the victim first opened the app 3 min 27 s after the last debit and first phoned the bank 6 min 32 s after it; recovery was 15% of reported value in early 2026 (BBS, 2026)"],
  ["SIM retention and expiry rules (The Bhutanese, n.d.-b)","No public SIM-replacement verification standard; \"blackout\" cases continue (BBS, 2026)"],
  ["Email de-linking under study (BBS, 2026)","Not yet reported as implemented"]]),
("h2","5.2 Recommendations"),
("p","What victims should preserve immediately. (1) Keep the phone on and unaltered: do not factory-reset, uninstall apps, delete messages or clear the call log, and keep the SIM. (2) Phone the bank at once and ask it to freeze the account and record the report time. (3) Photograph the SMS, call log and WhatsApp chat screens, noting the clock shown, and write down the scammer's numbers and what was said. (4) Do not reply to the scammer or call back. (5) Report to the RBP with the handset and ask investigators to take a forensic copy; the call log, SMS and chat databases are the artefacts reconstructed here. (6) Request the bank to preserve its own logs."),
("p","What banks should log and retain. For OTP requests and verification: time (synchronised clock), purpose, delivery status, and a hash of the code. For devices: device and installation identifiers, model, operating-system version, rooted or emulator flags, IP address and network, and the SIM or number used. For binding and resets: the old and new device, the channel, and the approval step. For transactions: session identifier, beneficiary and amount, and the risk score. Logs should be kept long enough to cover investigation delays, and the OTP message should state the purpose and amount, for example \"Code for NEW DEVICE on your account. Do not share. Bank staff never ask.\" A mandatory delay and a reduced transfer limit after any new-device binding, and a push alert to the old device that can deny the change, would have interrupted the 67-second window observed here."),
("p","What telecom operators should log. SIM-replacement requests with the identity checks performed, the channel and the staff or agent; IMSI and IMEI changes; SMS delivery receipts for bank sender identifiers; call detail records for fixed retention periods; and number-recycling records. Offering banks a way to query whether a SIM was recently replaced would close the blackout path."),
("h2","5.3 Limitations"),
("p","The experiment is synthetic. No real emulator or handset was used, the mock app was not compiled, WhatsApp messages were inserted rather than exchanged between accounts, Autopsy was not run, and the notification store used a custom format. Results therefore demonstrate the method and the reasoning about evidence, not the behaviour of any specific Android version, bank app or WhatsApp build. Real devices also introduce complications absent here, including encrypted databases, retention limits, deleted records, clock drift and user activity on the phone after the event. Bhutanese statistics are as reported by media and may have inconsistent dates. We did not interview victims, banks or police. Repeating the exercise on a real emulator would follow the same steps, replacing the generation script with adb extraction of /data/data."),
]))

# ---------------- 6 CONCLUSION
S(("6. Conclusion", [
("p","OTP-sharing takeovers leave a thin but usable trail on the victim's Android phone. In the reconstructed case SMS, call-log, WhatsApp, usage and notification artefacts together established the lure, the overlap of the scam call with both OTPs and both debits, the victim's absence from her own banking app, and her later admission. They could not show the decisive fact, the spoken disclosure of the code, nor the identity of the device that used it. Those facts need bank and telecom logs, which means victims must act and report quickly and banks and operators must record and retain device-level events. The reported changes by Bhutanese banks, notably disabling in-app resets, address the most obvious path, but the weakest remaining step in the chain, a code that a person can be talked into reading aloud, still decides the outcome. Clear purpose-bearing OTP messages, cooling-off limits after new-device binding, and quick access to server-side logs would offer the largest improvements. Future work should repeat the experiment on a live Android emulator with real WhatsApp traffic, test additional Android versions, and examine the SIM-replacement variant."),
]))

